Simple acute paronychia of the fingernail causing localized tenderness without significant fluctuance
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Lateral nail fold erythema, Localized edema, Exquisite direct tenderness

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-036
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe localized finger pain and swelling requiring assessment to rule out deep space flexor tenosynovitis or abscess requiring surgical drainage.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acutely swollen, extremely painful digit following minor trauma. Urgent physical assessment was strictly required prior to the next in-hours period to evaluate for a developing purulent abscess, rule out deep tendon sheath infection (which can rapidly destroy the tendon), and initiate appropriate conservative management.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My finger throbs incredibly hard right next to the nail, and I can't even bump it without intense pain.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes localized erythema surrounding the lateral nail fold and mild cutaneous edema of the proximal nail margin, worsening over the last 24 hours. Reports exquisite localized tenderness to direct palpation. Provides a history of recent minor trauma (cuticle biting). Denies any gross purulent fluid collection or fluctuance at this stage. Complete absence of fevers, chills, or spreading red streaks. Pain is described as a constant, dull throb.
Functional Status: Fully independent, but avoiding use of the affected digit.
Social/Safety Concerns: Impaired fine motor function due to pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 124/80 | HR: 82 | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, guarding the affected hand.
Physical Exam: Digit examination reveals localized erythema and edema of the lateral nail fold. Diagnostic triad confirmed: lateral nail fold erythema, localized edema, and exquisite direct tenderness. Intact underlying nail plate without dystrophy. Absence of gross purulent fluid collection or fluctuance. Normal capillary refill in the affected digit. Lack of ascending lymphangitis streaks. Normal active range of motion of the distal interphalangeal joint without pain on passive extension.
Environmental Observations: Nail biting habits evident on other digits.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient taking ibuprofen with partial relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Simple acute paronychia of the fingernail causing localized tenderness without significant fluctuance - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Infectious Flexor Tenosynovitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal active range of motion, absence of pain on passive extension, and lack of symmetric fusiform swelling of the entire digit.

Felon (Pulp Space Abscess) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Sparing of the distal finger pad/pulp space, with swelling localized strictly to the paronychial fold.

Herpetic Whitlow - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of grouped, fluid-filled vesicles on an erythematous base.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe localized digit pain and swelling, mimicking early deep space hand infections. An urgent face-to-face evaluation was mandatory to rule out flexor tenosynovitis, a surgical emergency that can lead to permanent loss of hand function if not treated immediately. The clinical examination confirmed the infection was localized to the superficial nail fold without fluctuance, averting an unnecessary emergency department referral for surgical incision and allowing for conservative home management.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed to perform warm water and Epsom salt soaks for 15 minutes, 3 to 4 times a day. Application of topical antibiotic ointment (e.g., mupirocin) following soaks.
Positive Behavioral Change: Substitute the habit of cuticle biting with applying a bitter-tasting nail polish or carrying a fidget object, addressing the mechanical trauma that introduced the oral bacteria into the skin barrier.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Epsom salts.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48 hours if swelling increases or if pus becomes visible under the skin.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if the entire finger becomes swollen and sausage-like, if pain shoots up the arm when you try to straighten the finger, or if you develop a fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is early localized infection; if a pocket of pus forms, it may require minor surgical drainage.
Clinical Course: Warm soaks will draw the inflammation to the surface. It may drain a small amount of pus spontaneously, after which the pain will rapidly improve.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if red streaks develop on your hand or arm, or if the pain becomes unbearable despite pain medication.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Biting the cuticle allowed normal skin bacteria to get trapped under the nail fold. The warm soaks increase blood flow to the area, helping your immune cells fight the infection and softening the skin to allow any trapped pus to drain naturally.
Actionable Steps: Soak the finger in very warm water for 15 minutes four times a day. Do not attempt to pop or cut the swelling yourself with unsterile tools.

SMS INFORMATION

